On Dec 30, 8:33 pm, hcobb <henry.c...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Why does this have zero game effect?

Why does _what_ have zero game effect?

> Here I am in perfect safety, hallucinating and confused, while I pull
> random potions out of my sack to dip a cursed unicorn horn into then
> quaff.

Not all potions have an immediately obvious effect.
Usually you can identify them anyway by the little
messages they give you, but messages tend to be
a little off from usual when you're hallucinating.

If you dip a horn in a potion of sickness (or fruit juice for
that matter) and quaff it, there will only be a noticeable
game effect if you're (just barely) hungry.  Quaffing
a potion of confusion or booze would usually have a
more obvious effect, but not so much if you're already
confused anyway.  Similarly, the horn will clear a potion
of confusion or blindness (and I think a third one too --
hallucination maybe) to water.  Quaffing a potion of
water ordinarily has very little game effect.  A potion
of see invisible has a game effect, but it can be less
than completely obvious at the best of times, and I
don't see how you could possibly notice it while
hallucinating, unless you have an invisible pet or
something.  A cursed potion of healing would only
have a noticeable effect if your hitpoints were not
already at maximum.  A cursed potion of restore
ability has no effect at all, and a non-cursed one
only has any effect if you've recently had one of
your stats reduced to below its maximum.  A
potion of sleeping doesn't do much if you've
eaten a few elf corpses.

> What is wrong with this picture?

The most obvious thing is, you have a unicorn
horn, but you're standing around quaffing random
potions while hallucinating, instead of applying
the unicorn horn.


